$ MacNeal & Harder (1985), Sec. 9 - pinched hemispherical shell.
$ Belytschko variant with an 18-deg hole at the apex (theta in [18,90]).
$ R = 10, t = 0.04, E = 6.825e7, nu = 0.3.
$ Free at the equator. Two pairs of opposite radial point loads P = 2.0 at
$ phi = 0, 90, 180, 270 deg on the equator. Pair (0, 180) pulls outward
$ in X; pair (90, 270) pulls inward in Y -- self-equilibrated.
$ Quarter model (phi in [0,90]) with symmetries:
$   phi=0  (XZ plane): Uy = Rx = Rz = 0
$   phi=90 (YZ plane): Ux = Ry = Rz = 0
$ Mesh 4 (theta) x 4 (phi) = 16 CQUAD4, 5x5 = 25 nodes.
$ Each of the two loaded equator points lies ON a symmetry plane, so the
$ quarter model carries half of P: 1.0 at node 5 (+X) and 1.0 at node 25 (-Y).
$ Reference radial displacement at node 5 for the 18-deg cut-out variant:
$ Ux = 0.0930 (Ko, Lee & Bathe 2017, Comput. Struct. 193, Sec. 3.7 / Table 15;
$ the frequently quoted 0.0924 is the CLOSED-hemisphere value, their Sec. 3.8).
$
SOL 101
CEND
TITLE = MacNeal-Harder pinched hemispherical shell (quarter, 18 deg hole)
SUBCASE 1
  LABEL = Two pairs of opposite radial unit loads at equator
  SPC = 1
  LOAD = 1
  DISPLACEMENT = ALL
BEGIN BULK
$ ---- solver parameters ----
$ Drilling-DOF stabilisation is not part of the benchmark definition and
$ solvers default it differently (MSC/Nastran 70.5 SOL 101: 0.0; MSC.Nastran
$ 2004+ and OpenJFEM: 100.0). On this deck that difference alone moves the
$ answer by 3.5%, so it is pinned here to keep both the accuracy comparison
$ and the same-deck parity comparison parameter-exact. See validation/README.md,
$ section "PARAM,K6ROT".
PARAM,K6ROT,0.
MAT1, 1, 6.825+7, , 0.3
PSHELL, 1, 1, 0.04, 1
$ ---- grids ----
$ Node ID = 5*j + i + 1; i in 0..4 (theta), j in 0..4 (phi).
$ theta = 18, 36, 54, 72, 90 deg.   phi = 0, 22.5, 45, 67.5, 90 deg.
$ x = R sin(theta) cos(phi), y = R sin(theta) sin(phi), z = R cos(theta).
$
$ j=0, phi=0:
GRID,  1, , 3.09017, 0.00000, 9.51057
